  (349, 'Fruit salad 1', 'South Indian', 'pan_indian', 'veg', true, 'fruit', '2', NULL, NULL, 'Community recipe.', 'Essentially a power-packed breakfast or snack bowl', 'Traditional', ARRAY['High in natural sugars for quick energy','but balanced with protein and micronutrients']::text[], ARRAY['½ cup Rajgira puffed','½ of cut Apple pcs','1 ripe mango','¼ cup of pomegranate kernels','2 cup milk','Pinch of garden cress (halim or alive)','2 dry dates (powdered)']::text[], ARRAY['Heat the Milk to a medrate temperature and put all the ingredients together. Allow the mixture to soak for 15 min and then serve.']::text[], ARRAY['breakfast','snack','lunch','dinner']::text[], 'bowl', 350, 575, 21, 109, 12, 10, 3.5, 420, 1.2, 140, true),
  (350, 'Black Coffee', 'South Indian', 'pan_indian', 'vegan', true, 'drink', NULL, '1 min', '3 min', 'Black coffee made from Indian filter coffee powder (typically a blend of Arabica and chicory) is a ritual across South India. The gentle roast and chicory addition give South Indian coffee its characteristic deep colour and slightly sweet bitterness that sets it apart from international coffee styles.', 'Do not pour boiling water; water that is too hot makes coffee bitter. Let the kettle rest 30 seconds after boiling.', 'Traditional', NULL, ARRAY['Coffee powder — 1 tsp','Hot water — 200 ml']::text[], ARRAY['Measure coffee powder into a cup.','Bring water to a boil (not a rolling boil — around 90°C is ideal).','Pour hot water over coffee.','Stir and serve black, or add a splash of milk if desired.']::text[], ARRAY['breakfast','lunch','dinner','snack']::text[], 'cup', 200, 5, 0.3, 0.7, 0, 0, 0.1, 5, 0, 5, true),
  (351, 'Thepla', 'Gujarati', 'west_indian', 'veg', true, 'staple', NULL, '15 min', '20 min', 'Thepla is Gujarat''s everyday flatbread and its ultimate travel companion — spiced with methi, eaten at breakfast, packed in dabbas and served on train journeys across India. A freshly made thepla with thick curd is a Gujarati morning in a single bite.', 'The dough should be soft; if it springs back on rolling, rest it 5 more minutes before rolling again.', 'Traditional', NULL, ARRAY['Whole wheat flour — 1 cup','Fresh fenugreek leaves — ½ cup, chopped','Besan (gram flour) — 2 tbsp','Ginger paste — 1 tsp','Turmeric — ¼ tsp','Red chilli powder — ½ tsp','Curd — 2 tbsp','Oil — 1 tsp','Salt to taste']::text[], ARRAY['Knead whole wheat flour, besan, methi, ginger paste, turmeric, chilli powder, curd and oil into a soft dough.','Divide into balls; roll into thin circles.','Cook on a hot tawa with a drizzle of oil until golden spots appear on both sides.','Serve warm with curd, pickle or chundo.']::text[], ARRAY['breakfast']::text[], 'piece', 60, 180, 5, 28, 6, 3, 1.8, 50, 0.05, 220, true),
  (353, 'Thalipeeth', 'Maharashtrian', 'west_indian', 'vegan', false, 'main', NULL, '10 min', '20 min', 'Thalipeeth is Maharashtra''s multi-grain flatbread — traditionally made with bhajni, a pre-roasted flour blend of jowar, bajra, wheat and besan. Nutritionally among the richest Indian flatbreads, it is the everyday breakfast of rural Maharashtra and a celebrated revival dish in urban health circles.', 'Work quickly — the multi-grain dough dries out faster than wheat dough; keep a bowl of water nearby.', 'Traditional', NULL, ARRAY['Jowar flour — ¼ cup','Bajra flour — ¼ cup','Besan — ¼ cup','Whole wheat flour — ¼ cup','Onion — 1 medium, chopped','Green chilli — 2, chopped','Coriander leaves — 2 tbsp','Turmeric — ¼ tsp','Oil — 2 tsp','Salt to taste']::text[], ARRAY['Mix all flours (jowar, bajra, besan, wheat) with onion, green chilli, coriander, turmeric and salt.','Add water gradually to form a soft, pliable dough.','Flatten a portion between wet palms into a round patty directly on a hot oiled griddle.','Cook covered on medium heat 4 minutes; flip and cook uncovered until done.','Serve hot with curd, chutney or thecha (green chilli relish).']::text[], ARRAY['breakfast']::text[], 'piece', 90, 220, 7, 35, 6, 4, 2.2, 45, 0, 250, true),
  (354, 'Misal Pav', 'Maharashtrian', 'west_indian', 'vegan', false, 'main', NULL, '10 min', '20 min', 'Misal Pav is Maharashtra''s most regional dish — every city has its own version. Pune''s is mild; Nashik''s is aggressive; Kolhapur''s is incendiary. The sprouted moth bean is the soul of the dish, making it a nutritionally dense street food that sustains market workers through a full morning.', 'Sprout the matki at home (soak 8 hrs, tie in cloth, rest 24 hrs) for maximum nutrition and flavour.', 'Traditional', NULL, ARRAY['Moth beans (matki) — 1 cup, sprouted','Bread rolls (pav) — 2','Onion — 1, chopped','Tomato — 1, chopped','Misal masala — 1 tbsp','Oil — 2 tsp','Farsan (crispy mix) — 2 tbsp','Coriander leaves — 2 tbsp','Salt to taste']::text[], ARRAY['Heat oil; add sprouted matki, onion and tomato; sauté 3 minutes.','Add misal masala and ½ cup water; cover and cook 10 minutes until sprouts are soft.','Season with salt. The rassa (gravy) should be thin and spicy.','Toast and lightly butter the pav.','Serve the misal in a bowl topped with onion, coriander, farsan and a squeeze of lemon; pav on the side.']::text[], ARRAY['breakfast','lunch']::text[], 'plate', 300, 350, 12, 52, 10, 8, 4, 90, 0, 650, true),
  (355, 'Neer Dosa', 'Konkani', 'west_indian', 'vegan', true, 'main', NULL, '10 min (plus 4 hr soak)', '15 min', 'Neer Dosa (water dosa) is Coastal Karnataka''s most ethereal creation — a diaphanous, snow-white, lace-edged crepe made from nothing but rice and water. Its delicacy is the point; it is the antithesis of the thick, crispy dosa. Konkani households serve it at breakfast and dinner with fish curry or coconut chutney.', 'Pour from height to create a naturally thin, holey crepe — ladling and spreading makes it too thick.', 'Traditional', NULL, ARRAY['Raw rice — 1 cup, soaked 4 hours','Salt to taste','Water — as needed','Oil — for greasing']::text[], ARRAY['Soak raw rice 4 hours; grind with water to an extremely thin, watery batter.','Add salt; the batter should pour like water.','Heat a non-stick or cast-iron griddle; grease lightly with oil.','Pour a ladleful of batter from a height — do not spread; let it flow naturally into a thin circle.','Cover and cook 2 minutes until the dosa is set and the edges lift. Do not flip.','Fold gently and serve immediately with coconut chutney.']::text[], ARRAY['breakfast']::text[], 'piece', 80, 120, 3, 22, 2, 1, 0.4, 10, 0, 150, true),
  (356, 'Akki Rotti', 'Karnataka', 'south_indian', 'vegan', false, 'main', NULL, '10 min', '20 min', 'Akki Rotti is Karnataka''s rice flour flatbread — a naturally gluten-free, protein-rich breakfast that North Karnataka households have eaten for generations. Unlike chapati rolled on a board, akki rotti is patted directly onto the hot tawa with a wet hand — a technique that takes practice.', 'Wet your palm generously before patting; a dry hand makes the rotti tear.', 'Traditional', NULL, ARRAY['Rice flour — 1 cup','Onion — 1 small, chopped','Green chilli — 2, chopped','Coriander leaves — 2 tbsp','Sesame seeds — 1 tsp','Grated fresh coconut — 2 tbsp','Salt to taste','Oil — 2 tsp']::text[], ARRAY['Mix rice flour with onion, green chilli, coriander, sesame, coconut and salt; add water to make a soft pliable dough.','Divide into balls. Wet your palm and flatten each ball directly on a hot oiled griddle.','Cook covered on medium heat 3–4 minutes; flip and cook uncovered until golden.','Serve hot with coconut chutney or saagu.']::text[], ARRAY['breakfast']::text[], 'piece', 120, 200, 4, 34, 6, 2, 1, 30, 0, 280, true),
  (357, 'Bajra Roti with Ghee and Jaggery', 'Rajasthani', 'north_indian', 'veg', true, 'staple', NULL, '10 min', '15 min', 'Bajra Roti with Ghee and Jaggery is Rajasthan''s oldest breakfast — pearl millet flatbread that sustained desert communities through harsh winters and scarce resources. Bajra is uniquely warming among all millets, making it the ideal grain for Rajasthan''s cold desert mornings.', 'Bajra dough dries out instantly; roll and cook each roti one at a time, never preparing in advance.', 'Traditional', NULL, ARRAY['Bajra flour (pearl millet) — 1 cup','Salt to taste','Hot water — as needed','Ghee — 1 tsp (for serving)','Jaggery — 1 small piece (for serving)']::text[], ARRAY['Knead bajra flour with salt and enough warm water to form a stiff dough (bajra does not bind like wheat).','Divide into balls; pat each into a thick round between palms — it will crack; patch as you go.','Cook on a hot dry tawa until charred spots appear on both sides and the roti feels firm.','Serve immediately with ghee and a small piece of jaggery on the side.']::text[], ARRAY['breakfast']::text[], 'piece', 110, 280, 5, 44, 9, 5, 3, 50, 0.05, 200, true),
  (358, 'Missi Roti', 'Rajasthani', 'north_indian', 'vegan', false, 'staple', NULL, '15 min', '20 min', 'Missi Roti is Rajasthan''s spiced flatbread — a protein-rich combination of whole wheat and besan that is the everyday breakfast of rural Rajasthan and increasingly celebrated in health-focused kitchens. Methi leaves add a slightly bitter edge that contrasts beautifully with rich dals.', 'The dough softens as the besan hydrates — let it rest 10 minutes before rolling for easier handling.', 'Traditional', NULL, ARRAY['Whole wheat flour — ½ cup','Besan (gram flour) — ½ cup','Fresh fenugreek leaves — ¼ cup, chopped','Onion — 1 small, chopped','Ajwain — ½ tsp','Red chilli powder — ½ tsp','Turmeric — ¼ tsp','Oil — 1 tsp','Salt to taste']::text[], ARRAY['Mix all flours (wheat, besan, methi leaves), onion, green chilli, ajwain, chilli powder, turmeric and salt.','Add water and oil gradually to form a soft, pliable dough.','Divide into balls; roll into medium-thick rounds.','Cook on a hot tawa with a drizzle of oil until golden on both sides.','Serve hot with curd, pickle and green chutney.']::text[], ARRAY['breakfast']::text[], 'piece', 90, 230, 9, 34, 7, 4, 2.5, 60, 0, 250, true),
  (359, 'Jolpan', 'Assamese', 'northeast_indian', 'veg', true, 'main', NULL, '10 min', '0 min', 'Jolpan is Assam''s traditional morning offering — poha, curd, jaggery and fresh coconut served during Bihu festivals. A no-cook dish that celebrates the valley''s abundance of rice and fresh produce.', 'Use thick curd and ripe banana for natural sweetness; adjust jaggery to taste.', 'Traditional', NULL, ARRAY['Poha (flattened rice) — 1 cup','Curd — ½ cup','Jaggery — 2 tbsp, grated','Sesame seeds — 1 tsp, roasted','Grated fresh coconut — 2 tbsp','Ripe banana — 1, sliced']::text[], ARRAY['Soak poha in water 2 minutes; drain well and squeeze out excess liquid.','Mix curd, jaggery and a pinch of salt until smooth.','Add drained poha, sesame seeds, grated coconut and banana.','Toss gently and serve at room temperature or slightly chilled.']::text[], ARRAY['breakfast','snack','lunch']::text[], 'bowl', 250, 280, 7, 48, 6, 2, 2, 140, 0.3, 60, true),
  (360, 'Pakhala Bhata', 'Odia', 'east_indian', 'veg', false, 'main', NULL, '5 min (plus overnight soak)', '0 min', 'Pakhala Bhata is Odisha''s ancient summer staple — fermented water-soaked rice eaten cold with raw vegetables and mustard oil. In the scorching Odia summers, pakhala cools the body from within. It is not just food but cultural identity — every year Pakhala Dibasa (Pakhala Day) is celebrated.', 'The overnight fermentation is what makes it pakhala — same-day soaking is just watered rice.', 'Traditional', NULL, ARRAY['Cooked rice — 1 cup','Water — 2 cups','Curd — ¼ cup','Cucumber — ½, diced','Onion — 1 small, sliced','Green chilli — 2, slit','Coriander leaves — 1 tbsp','Salt to taste']::text[], ARRAY['Soak cooked rice in 2 cups of water for 8 hours or overnight (pakhala ferments slightly).','In the morning, add curd and a pinch of salt; stir.','Prepare accompaniments: slice cucumber and onion, slit green chillies.','Serve the pakhala in a bowl topped with cucumber, onion, green chilli and a drizzle of mustard oil.','Eat by mixing everything together.']::text[], ARRAY['breakfast','lunch']::text[], 'bowl', 350, 220, 4, 42, 2, 1, 1, 80, 0.2, 320, true),
  (361, 'Muri with Coconut and Banana', 'Bengali', 'east_indian', 'vegan', true, 'snack', NULL, '8 min', '0 min', 'Muri with Coconut and Banana is a classic Bengali bhaater hotel breakfast — simple, nourishing and deeply comforting. Puffed rice softened in water is an ancient way to make rice digestible first thing in the morning, and the combination with coconut and jaggery provides balanced energy.', 'Soak muri for exactly 1 minute — longer and it turns to paste; shorter and it stays hard.', 'Traditional', NULL, ARRAY['Puffed rice (muri) — 2 cups','Grated fresh coconut — 3 tbsp','Ripe banana — 1, sliced','Jaggery — 1 tbsp, grated','Mustard oil — ½ tsp','Green chilli — 1, finely chopped','Salt — a pinch']::text[], ARRAY['Soak puffed rice in water for 1 minute; drain and squeeze gently — it should be soft but not soggy.','In a bowl, combine the softened muri with grated coconut, sliced banana and jaggery.','Add a pinch of salt and a few drops of mustard oil; mix gently.','Serve immediately as a morning snack.']::text[], ARRAY['breakfast','snack']::text[], 'bowl', 150, 220, 4, 42, 5, 2, 1.2, 30, 0, 180, true),
  (362, 'Radhaballabhi', 'Bengali', 'east_indian', 'vegan', true, 'snack', NULL, '20 min (plus soak)', '25 min', 'Radhaballabhi is a treasured Bengali breakfast puri — a maida puri stuffed with spiced urad dal filling, named after the deity Radha and Ballabh (another name for Krishna). Served at Durga Puja and special morning meals, it is always paired with cholar dal.', 'Fry on medium heat, not high; a very hot oil seals the surface before the inside cooks through.', 'Traditional', NULL, ARRAY['Maida (all-purpose flour) — 1 cup','Urad dal — ½ cup, soaked','Fennel seeds — ½ tsp','Ginger — ½ inch, grated','Green chilli — 2, chopped','Oil — for frying','Salt to taste']::text[], ARRAY['Drain soaked urad dal; grind coarsely with fennel, ginger, green chilli and salt to a thick filling.','Make a stiff dough with maida and a little oil and water.','Roll small rounds, place filling in the centre, seal and flatten into thick discs.','Deep-fry in medium-hot oil, turning frequently, until puffed and golden.','Serve hot with cholar dal or aloo dum.']::text[], ARRAY['breakfast']::text[], 'piece', 90, 280, 9, 42, 8, 4, 2.2, 40, 0, 280, true),
  (363, 'Puttu with Kadala Curry', 'Kerala', 'south_indian', 'vegan', false, 'main', NULL, '20 min (plus overnight soak)', '40 min', 'Puttu with Kadala Curry is Kerala''s defining breakfast combination — steamed rice and coconut cylinders paired with a dark, robust black chickpea curry. The puttu''s neutral starchiness and the kadala''s earthy depth create a balance that Kerala mornings are built around.', 'The rice flour texture for puttu must feel like wet sand when squeezed — too wet and it steams into a solid block.', 'Traditional', NULL, ARRAY['Rice flour — 1 cup','Grated fresh coconut — ½ cup','Black chickpeas (kadala) — 1 cup, soaked overnight','Onion — 1, chopped','Tomato — 1, chopped','Coconut milk — ½ cup','Garam masala — 1 tsp','Turmeric — ¼ tsp','Oil — 2 tsp','Salt to taste']::text[], ARRAY['Steam puttu: mix rice flour with water (crumbly texture), layer with coconut in a puttu maker and steam 8 minutes.','Soak black chickpeas overnight; pressure cook until very tender.','Heat oil; sauté onion until golden. Add tomato, turmeric and garam masala; cook 5 min.','Add coconut milk and cooked kadala; simmer 15 minutes until thick.','Serve puttu broken over the kadala curry — they are meant to be mixed.']::text[], ARRAY['breakfast']::text[], 'plate', 350, 380, 14, 58, 10, 8, 4.5, 110, 0, 420, true),
  (364, 'Idiyappam with Coconut Milk', 'Kerala', 'south_indian', 'vegan', true, 'main', NULL, '10 min', '15 min', 'Idiyappam with Coconut Milk is Kerala''s most delicate breakfast — steamed string hoppers made fresh each morning, eaten with sweetened coconut milk drawn from freshly grated coconut. In Malabar households it is a Sunday ritual; in Travancore, an everyday comfort.', 'The batter must be smooth and flowing — too thick and the strings break; too thin and they merge together.', 'Traditional', NULL, ARRAY['Rice flour — 1 cup','Coconut milk — 1 cup','Jaggery — 2 tbsp','Cardamom powder — ½ tsp','Salt — a pinch','Oil — for greasing']::text[], ARRAY['Mix rice flour with a pinch of salt and water to form a smooth, thin batter.','Press batter through an idiyappam press or chakli maker into circular nest shapes on steamer plates.','Steam 8–10 minutes until cooked through.','Warm coconut milk with jaggery and cardamom until jaggery dissolves; do not boil.','Serve idiyappam with the warm sweetened coconut milk poured over.']::text[], ARRAY['breakfast']::text[], 'plate', 250, 280, 5, 48, 8, 2, 1.5, 40, 0, 120, true),
  (366, 'Mooli Paratha', 'Punjabi', 'north_indian', 'vegan', false, 'main', NULL, '20 min', '20 min', 'Mooli Paratha is Punjab''s winter breakfast staple — a whole wheat flatbread stuffed with fresh white radish that is grated, squeezed and spiced. Eaten through the cold months when mooli is at its sweetest, it is one of the great comfort breakfasts of North India.', 'Squeezing out all the mooli water is critical — wet filling makes the paratha soggy and difficult to roll.', 'Traditional', NULL, ARRAY['Whole wheat flour — 1 cup','White radish (mooli) — 2 medium, grated','Green chilli — 2, finely chopped','Ginger — ½ inch, grated','Ajwain — ¼ tsp','Cumin powder — ½ tsp','Oil — 2 tsp','Salt to taste']::text[], ARRAY['Grate mooli; add salt and rest 5 minutes. Squeeze out all the water firmly.','Mix squeezed mooli with green chilli, ginger, ajwain and cumin powder.','Make a dough with wheat flour, salt and water; rest 15 min.','Stuff mooli filling into dough balls; roll into parathas.','Cook with oil on a hot tawa until golden on both sides.']::text[], ARRAY['breakfast']::text[], 'piece', 120, 250, 7, 36, 8, 3, 2.2, 40, 0, 300, true),
  (367, 'Kozhukattai', 'Tamil', 'south_indian', 'vegan', true, 'snack', NULL, '20 min', '15 min', 'Kozhukattai is Tamil Nadu''s steamed dumpling — known as modak in Maharashtra and offered to Lord Ganesha during Vinayagar Chaturthi. The rice flour outer skin becomes gloriously translucent when steamed, revealing the coconut-jaggery filling within.', 'Keep the dough warm and covered while shaping — cold dough cracks at the edges when you bring them together.', 'Traditional', NULL, ARRAY['Rice flour — 1 cup','Grated fresh coconut — 4 tbsp','Jaggery — 3 tbsp','Cardamom powder — ½ tsp','Salt — a pinch']::text[], ARRAY['Mix rice flour with a pinch of salt and coconut; gradually add hot water to form a soft dough.','Add jaggery and cardamom to the grated coconut filling; mix well.','Take a small ball of dough; flatten in your palm; place filling in centre.','Bring edges together and shape into a smooth dumpling (modak shape).','Steam for 10 minutes until the outer skin turns translucent.','Serve warm with a drizzle of ghee.']::text[], ARRAY['breakfast','snack']::text[], 'serving', 100, 180, 4, 34, 4, 2, 0.8, 20, 0, 80, true)
